 the  same product on AliExpress only cost ten  dollars and an even better Hub on  Alibaba only costs 10 to 20 cents and  I'm not cherry picking a dongle either  there are dozens of dongles on Alibaba  that cost just 10 to 20 cents now  granted you do have to make a minimum  order of 200 units on Alibaba but at 20  cents a piece that's only forty dollars  even with international shipping you can  easily get your hands on 200 of these  for less than 100 bucks which is a net  unit cost of less than 50 cents if you  were an Amazon Seller buying thousands  of these you could probably Source them  for 25 cents per unit so when customers  are paying 25 dollars for a product that  only costs 25 cents it's no wonder why  the product does not stack up to their  quality expectations efficient market  hypothesis would tell you that excess  profit margins usually Disappear With  time as more competitors enter the  market these downloads however have  plenty of competition yet all of them  cost 20 20 to 30 dollars so where in the  world is all this money going if not  into the product itself  one of the biggest issues plaguing  Amazon's private label business is  simply how the entire service is  structured usually most private label  businesses use something called an  Amazon FBA this is when you Source  Products from Alibaba and then shipped  inventory directly to Amazon Amazon will  take care of sorting their products  Distributing them Across America and  shipping them to customers with their  insane time frames honestly the fact  that Amazon is able to handle so many  products and ship them to so many people  so quickly is truly a logistics Marvel  but it's also extremely expensive not  only does Amazon charge us the customers  for Amazon Prime or shipping fees but  they also charge Sellers and Amazon FBA  fee right now the absolute smallest fee  that Amazon charges sellers is 3.7 cents  per unit and that's where extremely  small and light objects to be  categorized as a small standard size the  shortest side of the given product has  to be less than three quarters of an  inch fortunately the USB PC cable that  we were looking at would fall under this  definition but there's plenty of small  items that don't take this tiny pot for  example which only measures 2 inches on  every side most of us would classify  this as a small item but Amazon would  actually classify it as a large standard  item and if the product has some heft to  it as in 2-3 pounds sellers can easily  end up paying six dollars and eight  cents per unit and that's simply for a  small item if you're actually selling a  medium-sized item it'll be classified as  an oversized atom for which Amazon will  charge a minimum of 10 per unit sellers  have to pay these fees for every unit  they sell from the back end and they of  course just pass on these fees to us the  customers so really we're paying for  Logistics twice for your average 20  product three to six dollars goes to  paying the Amazon FBA fee and to give in  that most products do not fit within the  small standard size the average fee is  skewed towards six dollars for  Simplicity we'll call it five dollars  average spoiler alert that's just the  first fee Amazon also charges sellers a  referral fee the reason that Amazon FBA  businesses are able to get off the  ground so quickly is because Amazon  already has a massive customer base and  to gain access to this customer base you  have to pay Amazon at eight to fifteen  percent of your total sale price so for  a 20 product this works out to anywhere  between 1.60 cents and three dollars  while estimated in the middle and to  call it two dollars and thirty Cents  Tack this onto the FBA fee and now we're  looking at a total of seven dollars and  thirty cents per unit moving on we have  inventory storage fees this fee is  pretty self-explanatory Amazon charges  sellers to be able to store their  products at Amazon warehouses between  January and September this fee isn't too  bad coming in at 83 cents per cubic feet  but during the holiday months this fee  triples to 2.40 cents per cubic feet on  top of all this Amazon also charges a 40  per month fee for simply being a  professional seller on Amazon on that  wraps up all of the required fees that  all Amazon FBA sellers have to deal with  but some sellers may be subject to  additional fees as well for example when  you ship products to Amazon they want  each product labeled and barcoded in a  specific way you can either pair  supplier to do this which is definitely  a cheaper option or you can pay Amazon  to do this if you want Amazon to do this  or if your supplier did it wrong Amazon  will charge you a minimum of 1.25 cents  per unit additionally sellers are also  subject to a product return fee and  inventory removal fee and other  penalties when you add all of this  together you realize that for an average  20 product the seller is paying Amazon  10 off the top line for Logistics and  seller fees in other words fifty percent  of the products purchase price is not  even going towards the product itself  it's going towards making the part  available on Amazon and then getting it  to you now again I'm not trying to back  on Amazon Logistics especially the way  Amazon closet is expensive but as a  customer just know that this is what  you're really paying for  10 fee for a 20 product is no dot quite  expensive but that still leaves ten  dollars the seller should be able to  spend five dollars on the product and  then profit five dollars right that  would be a win-win scenario Amazon gets  their fees the seller makes their  profits and the customer gets at least a  decent product but the thing that you're  forgetting about is marketing some  bigger private label Brands go out and  run Google ads Facebook ads and even  YouTube ads but by far the most  effective type of marketing is Amazon  ads whenever you search for something on  Amazon you know how the top search  results say sponsored well these aren't  Amazon ads and they're extremely  expensive because they're extremely  effective if you were to see an ad  before this video maybe one in a  thousand of you would actually go buy  the product but whenever you put a  product at the top of Amazon search in  front of people who are already  searching for the product and are ready  to buy the conversion rate goes through  the roof the average conversion rate  across Amazon minus 10 to 15 percent and  the average conversion rate across Prime  members is a mind-boggling at 74 to  capture these insane conversions however  you have to be at the top of Amazon  search sixty percent of all Amazon  sellers go to the first three listings  and eighty percent go to the first page  so if you're launching a new Amazon  product you basically have to pay for  Amazon ads to get any sort of traction  the average cost per click across Amazon  is 89 cents in other words every time  someone clicks on a sponsored listing  the seller has to pay Amazon at 89 cents  and that's just the average I used this  on Amazon myself in moderately  competitive niches and to secure the  first spot in those niches it cost three  dollars per click and it that was back  in 2019 with the influx of online  shopping due to the pandemic I wouldn't  be surprised if that number was closer  to five dollars per click today  especially for something as competitive  as USBC dongles but even if we estimate  conservatively and College at 89 cents  per click if you have a conversion rate  of 10 percent it's gonna cost you 8.90  to move a single unit combine this with  the FBA fees and you'll be paying Amazon  at 18.90 per unit and that's not even  including the cost of the product  realistically though there's no way  you'll only be paying 89 cents per click  especially when you launch if you want  your listing to appear within the top  two response you're likely gonna have to  pay three dollars per click and even if  you have a conversion rate of 20 that  works out to Fifteen dollars per unit  combine this with the FBA fee and your  up to 25 per unit you're five dollars in  the Reddit and you haven't even paid for  the product yet and this is why private  ever products on Amazon are so garbage  given how much sellers have to pay for  FBA and how much they have to pay for  marketing they have no money left to  actually pay for the product itself you  can't feel too bad for the sellers  though because if they do make it to the  other side and rank their product highly  they end up making bank at this point  they can substantially scale back on the  Amazon ads to maybe the average of 89  cents per click if they maintain the 20  conversion rate this comes out to 4.45  cents per click combine this with the  FBA Fiat and they're up to 14.45 cents  per unit if we say the product costs  2.50 per unit they're all on cost is  roughly 17 per unit at a sale price of  twenty dollars they'll be clearing three  dollars on every unit and the top  sellers in the popular nieces generally  sell well over 10 000 units per month  which works out at thirty thousand  dollars per month in profit off of just  one product if you replicate the success  with the 10 products you'll be making  3.6 million dollars per year I think you  can see why Amazon FBA is such a popular  business but in terms of the quality of  their product other times they finish  paying Amazon and themselves there's  nothing left to spend on the product  itself and this is why private label  products on Amazon are so garbage  foreign  the number one thing that we can take  away is that the vast majority of the  purchase price goes directly to Amazon  for a 20 product it's extremely common  for 75 of the proceeds to go to Amazon  between the selling fees at the FDA fees  and the marketing fees but the ironic  part is that Amazon isn't profiting from  any of this either in fact they're  literally losing money within the first  six months of 2022 Amazon lost 5 billion  on their retail business and the main  reason for this is that it's  extraordinarily expensive to pay for  hundreds of thousands of warehouse  workers delivery drivers Pilots planes  trucks ships factories warehouses boxes  boxing materials and everything else  that goes along with Logistics by the  end of it neither Amazon Northern  employees are left with that much money  and customers are left with the trash  products the only people who come out on  top are Amazon shareholders and the  select FBA sellers who make it to the  other side the obvious solution to this  conundrum is to simply spend less money  on Logistics and more money on the  products themselves but with our  increasingly short attention spans this  is simply unfeasible so maybe the  problem isn't Amazon or FBA sellers or  their products themselves maybe the real  problem is actually just our insatiable  desire for instant gratification but  that's just what I think have you had a  bad experience with the Private Label  Amazon product comment that down below  also drop a like if you hoped that  Amazon does something about the quality  issue and of course consider checking  out our Discord Community suggested  video ideas and consider subscribing to  see more questions logically answered  but until then I'm Hari and I'll see you  guys on the next one
 

